<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1929 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Horst-Wessel-Lied</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The anthem of Hitler's Nazi Party from 1930 to 1945; it has been banned in Germany and Austria since the war.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Horst Wessel</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>not known (officially credited to Horst Wessel; the melody is older)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Nazi Party (NSDAP) and its paramilitary SA; Nazi Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Berlin, Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words 1929; party anthem 1930–1945</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Horst Wessel (d. 1930), published 1929; melody older and of disputed origin)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horst-wessel-lied">Horst-Wessel-Lied</a>&quot; was the anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> (NSDAP) from 1930 to 1945, and from 1933 to 1945 the Nazi regime made it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s second national anthem, sung after the first stanza of the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a>&quot;. Its words were written in 1929 by Horst Wessel, a Sturmführer of the party's paramilitary Sturmabteilung (SA, the &quot;Brownshirts&quot;) in the Friedrichshain district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and were published in September 1929 in Der Angriff, the Berlin party newspaper owned and run by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels">Joseph Goebbels</a>. Wessel wrote songs for the SA in conscious imitation of the Communist Red Front Fighters' League, to provoke its members into attacking his men and to keep up their spirits. On Goebbels's orders he led SA attacks into the Fischerkiez, a very poor Berlin district; in one, outside the tavern the local Communist Party used as its headquarters, five Communists were injured, four seriously.</p><p>On 14 January 1930 Wessel was shot and seriously wounded at his lodgings by two Communist Party members, one of them Albrecht Höhler, after a dispute with his landlady over rent; he died on 23 February of blood poisoning. Höhler was sentenced to six years in prison; after the Nazis took power, the SA took him out of prison under false pretences in September 1933 and shot him dead. Goebbels, who had earlier tried and failed to present the suicide of an SA man as a Communist murder, built Wessel into the movement's central martyr from a mixture of fact and fiction. He declared Wessel's march, renamed the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horst-wessel-lied">Horst-Wessel-Lied</a>&quot;, to be the party anthem. The SA sang it at Wessel's funeral on 1 March 1930, which the party filmed and turned into a major propaganda event, and afterwards at party functions and in street parades. The historian Oliver Rathkolb describes it as created as a counterweight to &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-internationale">The Internationale</a>&quot;.</p><p>After Hitler became chancellor in January 1933, a law of 19 May 1933 made the song a national symbol. A regulation of the next year required the raised-arm Nazi salute while its first stanza was sung, and Hitler fixed the tempo at which it had to be played; Nazi leaders sing it at the close of Leni Riefenstahl's film Triumph of the Will (1935). Officials policed its use: a bandleader who wrote a jazz version was forced to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and in 1933 a young Nazi official switched off an unusual arrangement played at a party given by Martha Dodd, daughter of the US ambassador. Some Protestant churches that had accepted Goebbels's Wessel cult, modelled on Christian martyrs, played it in worship. Wessel was officially credited with the music too, though German critics pointed out before 1933 that the melody had a long history; after 1933 such criticism was taboo and risked severe punishment. Its most likely direct source is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> navy song, the &quot;Königsberg-Lied&quot;; in 1936 the critic Alfred Weidemann traced the tune to a song of 1865 by Peter Cornelius, who called it a Viennese folk tune.</p><p>Fascist movements elsewhere took up the tune in the 1930s and 1940s: the British Union of Fascists, the Spanish Falange, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of French Volunteers Against Bolshevism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the All-Russian Fascist Organisation of émigrés in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lapua-movement">Lapua Movement</a> and Patriotic People's Movement. Its opponents parodied it: before 1933 Communists and Social Democrats sang rival versions in their street battles with the SA, versions banned once those parties were prohibited; under the regime underground parodies mocked the corruption of Nazi leaders; Brecht and Eisler wrote the &quot;Kälbermarsch&quot; (Calves' March) for Brecht's play Schweik in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> (1943); and Oliver Wallace's &quot;Der Fuehrer's Face&quot; for a 1942 Donald Duck cartoon was Spike Jones's first hit record.</p><p>With the end of the Nazi regime in May 1945 the song was banned. Its words and tune are illegal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> today, except for artistic or educational purposes. In early 2011 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> police investigation led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> and Apple to remove it from their stores. Far-right groups still use it: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> an adaptation is an unofficial anthem of several far-right movements, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> the Golden Dawn party has sung it with Greek words at its gatherings. A unit of the Chilean Navy's marine commandos uses the melody with other words. Composers have quoted it to evoke the regime, among them Karlheinz Stockhausen in Hymnen (1967) and Lukas Foss in his Elegy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-frank">Anne Frank</a> (1989).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A marching song of the Nazi SA, presenting the party's street fighters and its dead as a cause to follow; aggressive and martial.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-caution" data-say>These are the words of the anthem of the Nazi Party, printed as the historical record for study. They glorify the SA, the party's paramilitary force, and call for the Nazi flag to fly over every street. Since 1945 the song and its tune have been banned in Germany and Austria, outside teaching, research and the arts.</p><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die Fahne hoch! Die Reihen fest geschlossen!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Raise the flag! The ranks tightly closed!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">SA marschiert mit ruhig festem Schritt.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The SA marches with calm, steady step.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Kam'raden, die Rotfront und Reaktion erschossen,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Comrades shot by the Red Front and reactionaries</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Marschier'n im Geist in unser'n Reihen mit.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>March in spirit within our ranks.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die Straße frei den braunen Bataillonen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Clear the streets for the brown battalions,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die Straße frei dem Sturmabteilungsmann!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Clear the streets for the storm division man!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Es schau'n aufs Hakenkreuz voll Hoffnung schon Millionen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Millions are looking upon the swastika full of hope,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Der Tag für Freiheit und für Brot bricht an!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The day of freedom and of bread dawns!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Zum letzten Mal wird Sturmalarm geblasen!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the last time, the call to arms is sounded!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Zum Kampfe steh'n wir alle schon bereit!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the fight, we all stand prepared!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Schon flattern Hitlerfahnen über allen Straßen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Already Hitler's banners fly over all streets.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die Knechtschaft dauert nur noch kurze Zeit!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The time of bondage will last but a little while now!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Horst Wessel (1929), as quoted in English Wikipedia. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horst-Wessel-Lied" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: English translation as given in English Wikipedia (CC BY-SA). Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horst-Wessel-Lied" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Marching Song of the British Union of Fascists</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1930s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, words modelled on it</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Camisa azul (Falange)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hrvatski Stijeg (Jan Zadravec)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Song of the Patriotic People's Movement (Finland, translated by Otto Al'Antila)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1930s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kälbermarsch (Bertolt Brecht and Hanns Eisler, in Schweik in the Second World War)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1943</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Der Fuehrer's Face (Oliver Wallace, recorded by Spike Jones)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1942</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody to a similar melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Chant des Waffen SS (Pierre Dac)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hymnen (Karlheinz Stockhausen)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1967</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Elegy for Anne Frank (Lukas Foss)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1989</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Himno de la Agrupación de Comandos IM no. 51 (Chilean Navy)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Königsberg-Lied (German Imperial Navy song)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">World War I</span><span class="sg-chain-h">most likely source of the melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Song by Peter Cornelius (&quot;Viennese folk tune&quot;)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1865</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source melody according to Alfred Weidemann (1936)</span></li></ul></section>
